import{_ as t,c as o,o as d,ae as a}from"./chunks/framework.DrOG_Kkp.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"API Changes","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/api-changes.md","filePath":"reference/api-changes.md"}'),r={name:"reference/api-changes.md"};function c(n,e,s,i,h,p){return d(),o("div",null,e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="api-changes" tabindex="-1">API Changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#api-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;API Changes&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Changes to the APIs Structures exposes, by release: the Java and TypeScript services, the REST API and the OpenAPI spec it is described by. Each release&#39;s notes include its section.</p><p>In the paths below, <code>{app}</code> is the application id and <code>{structure}</code> the structure name, both lowercase, under the configured <code>openApiPath</code> (<code>/api/</code> by default) or <code>openApiAdminPath</code> (<code>/admin/api/</code>). In operationIds, <code>&lt;S&gt;</code> is the structure name with its first letter capitalized.</p><h2 id="_3-9-0" tabindex="-1">3.9.0 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-9-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.9.0&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Writes that refresh before returning, so they can be searched straight away whatever the refresh settings say. See <a href="./structures-server-config.html#choosing-a-refresh-mode">Choosing a refresh mode</a> for when to use them.</p><h3 id="added" tabindex="-1">Added <a class="header-anchor" href="#added" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Added&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Services.</strong> <code>@kinotic/structures-api</code> 3.7.0 and the Java <code>structures-api</code> 3.9.0:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Methods</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>JsonEntitiesService</code> (Java), <code>IEntitiesService</code> / <code>IEntityService</code> (TypeScript)</td><td><code>saveSync</code>, <code>updateSync</code>, <code>deleteByIdSync</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>AdminJsonEntitiesService</code> (Java), <code>IAdminEntitiesService</code> / <code>IAdminEntityService</code> (TypeScript)</td><td><code>deleteByIdSync</code> (by tenant and entity id)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>They take the same values as <code>save</code>, <code>update</code> and <code>deleteById</code>, plus a <code>waitFor</code> flag, and return the same values. They are allowed or denied by the same policies. By default they force a refresh; with <code>waitFor</code> they wait for the next scheduled refresh instead, so they take up to the index&#39;s <code>refresh_interval</code> longer, and fail with a timeout, with the write applied, if that interval is longer than the Elasticsearch socket timeout (see <a href="./structures-server-config.html">waitFor</a>). In TypeScript <code>waitFor</code> is an optional last argument; in Java it is a <code>boolean</code> before the <code>Participant</code>. Code that implements these interfaces itself, rather than using the classes Structures provides, has to add the new methods.</p><p><strong>REST operations</strong>, for every structure, all in the OpenAPI spec:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Method and path</th><th>operationId</th><th>Same as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/save-sync</code></td><td><code>saveSync&lt;S&gt;</code></td><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}</code> (<code>save&lt;S&gt;</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/update-sync</code></td><td><code>updateSync&lt;S&gt;</code></td><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/update</code> (<code>update&lt;S&gt;</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /api/{app}/{structure}/{id}/sync</code></td><td><code>deleteSync&lt;S&gt;</code></td><td><code>DELETE /api/{app}/{structure}/{id}</code> (<code>delete&lt;S&gt;</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /admin/api/{app}/{structure}/{tenantId}/{id}/sync</code></td><td><code>deleteSync&lt;S&gt;Admin</code></td><td><code>DELETE /admin/api/{app}/{structure}/{tenantId}/{id}</code> (<code>delete&lt;S&gt;Admin</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each takes an optional <code>waitFor</code> query parameter (<code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, default <code>false</code>), described in the spec; any other value is a 400. The admin operation is in the spec only for structures with multi-tenant selection, as its plain twin is. GraphQL has no new operations.</p><h3 id="changed" tabindex="-1">Changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changed&quot;">​</a></h3><p>These change the OpenAPI spec, and two REST routes, to describe what the server really does. <strong>Clients generated from the spec will change when regenerated</strong>, as described for each; clients that call the REST API directly see only the 406 change.</p><p><strong>Responses with no body.</strong> These operations answer 200 with an empty body and no <code>Content-Type</code>, but the spec described the response as <code>application/json</code> without a schema. It now describes them without content, which is how OpenAPI says there is no body. The server&#39;s responses are unchanged.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Method and path</th><th>operationId</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DELETE /api/{app}/{structure}/{id}</code></td><td><code>delete&lt;S&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/delete/by-query</code></td><td><code>delete&lt;S&gt;ByQuery</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/bulk</code></td><td><code>bulkSave&lt;S&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/bulk-update</code></td><td><code>bulkUpdate&lt;S&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /api/{app}/{structure}/util/sync</code></td><td><code>sync&lt;S&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /admin/api/{app}/{structure}/{tenantId}/{id}</code></td><td><code>delete&lt;S&gt;Admin</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /admin/api/{app}/{structure}/delete/by-query</code></td><td><code>delete&lt;S&gt;ByQueryAdmin</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The two admin operations are in the spec only for structures with multi-tenant selection. The new <code>deleteSync&lt;S&gt;</code> and <code>deleteSync&lt;S&gt;Admin</code> are documented the same way.</p><p><em>Breaking for regenerated clients:</em> these methods now return nothing (<code>void</code>) instead of an untyped value, so code that used their result no longer compiles. Generators that parsed the old documented JSON, such as <code>typescript-fetch</code>, could fail on every successful call to them; those clients now work.</p><p><strong>Error responses.</strong> Errors (400, 401, 403 and 500 on every operation, and 409 where documented) answer <code>{&quot;error&quot;: &quot;&lt;message&gt;&quot;}</code> as <code>application/json</code>, but the spec described them without content. They now reference a new <code>ErrorResponse</code> schema with a string <code>error</code> property. A 404 for an entity that isn&#39;t found has an empty body and is still documented without content. The server&#39;s responses are unchanged. An application with a structure or entity type named <code>ErrorResponse</code> keeps that schema for it; its errors then reference <code>ErrorResponse2</code>, or the next free name.</p><p><em>For regenerated clients:</em> they gain an <code>ErrorResponse</code> model, and can read the error message instead of an undocumented body.</p><p><strong><code>bulk</code> and <code>bulk-update</code> accept any <code>Accept</code> header.</strong> <code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/bulk</code> and <code>POST /api/{app}/{structure}/bulk-update</code> answered 406 to a request whose <code>Accept</code> header didn&#39;t include <code>application/json</code>, although they answer with no body. They now answer it like any other request, as the deletes do.</p><h3 id="compatibility" tabindex="-1">Compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compatibility&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Older servers:</strong> clients that call the new methods or operations need a 3.9.0 server; an older one answers method not found, or 404 for the REST operations. Older clients work with a 3.9.0 server unchanged.</li><li><strong>Rolling upgrades:</strong> while servers of both versions run, a call to a new method or operation can reach an older server, so start using them once every server runs 3.9.0.</li><li><strong>Configuration and data:</strong> two new settings, <code>elasticNumberOfShards</code> and <code>elasticNumberOfReplicas</code>, set the shards and replicas of every index Structures creates (see <a href="./structures-server-config.html">the server configuration</a>). Their defaults, 3 and 2, are the counts used before, except for the <code>migration_history</code> index, which a new cluster now creates with them instead of Elasticsearch&#39;s default of 1 and 1. Existing indexes are not changed. Going back to 3.8.0 is safe; only callers of the new methods and operations stop working, and indexes created in the meantime keep the counts they were created with.</li><li><strong>Generated clients:</strong> regenerating from the 3.9.0 spec changes the methods listed under Changed, as described there. Clients generated from an older spec work against a 3.9.0 server as they did before.</li></ul><h3 id="known-issue" tabindex="-1">Known issue <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-issue" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known issue&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A new operationId can repeat one that another structure already has: <code>saveSync</code> for a structure <code>Job</code> and <code>save</code> for a structure <code>SyncJob</code> are both <code>saveSyncJob</code>. The REST paths always differ, since each includes the structure name, so every operation works; only clients generated from the spec are affected, as they already were by repeats such as <code>Job</code>&#39;s countByQuery and <code>JobByQuery</code>&#39;s count (<code>countJobByQuery</code>).</p>',25)]))}const y=t(r,[["render",c]]);export{u as __pageData,y as default};
